  "worked_solution": "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -16 & -3\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,\u27fa\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{\u23de}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
